 ### Conservative Rasterization via Edge Expansion
-**Definition:** A technique to fix 'holes' introduced by switching projection directions in different primitives. Standard rasterization invokes the pixel shader only if a primitive covers the pixel center; this method expands primitive edges to ensure all pixels touched by the primitive are covered [S1, unofficial].
+**Definition:** A technique to fix 'holes' introduced by switching projection directions in different primitives [S1, unofficial]. Standard rasterization invokes the pixel shader only if a primitive covers the pixel center; this method expands primitive edges to ensure all pixels touched by the primitive are covered [S1, unofficial].
 **Step:** Expand primitive edges in the Geometry Shader to ensure all pixels touched by the primitive are covered by the rasterizer [S1, unofficial].
-**Pitfall:** Edge expansion causes extra pixels to be shaded, particularly for thin triangles, leading to unnecessary pixel shader calls. These extra voxels must be culled later using voxel-primitive intersection tests [S1, unofficial].
